\section{Choose Evidence That Answers the Question}

Simulation compares behavior on chosen traces. Property checking asks whether all behaviors in a model obey a specification. Equivalence compares two representations, not their correctness against an independent requirement. For example, synthesis can accurately preserve an RTL counter that saturates at the wrong value. Conversely a counter that meets its specification can differ from a reference's defined reset trace. Name the compared ports, the reset and unknown-state model, and the permissible input sequences before calling either result a pass.

The \emph{Handbook of Satisfiability} distinguishes bounded model checking, which unfolds a transition relation for a chosen number of steps, from proof techniques such as induction. A bounded counterexample disproves the checked property under the model. No counterexample to depth \(k\) excludes only those modeled traces up to \(k\), unless another argument establishes completeness. Induction requires both a base case and a sufficiently strong step; strengthening a property can be necessary to exclude impossible states.

