\section{Check a Saturating Counter at Its Boundaries}

The following IEEE SystemVerilog \cite{ieee1800systemverilog} uses a synchronous reset. All values of the three-bit counter fit in the specified state space \(0\) through \(7\).

\begin{verbatim}
module sat_count(input logic clk, rst, en,
                 output logic [2:0] q);
    always_ff @(posedge clk)
        if (rst) q <= 3'd0;
        else if (en && q != 3'd7) q <= q + 3'd1;
endmodule
\end{verbatim}

An independent scoreboard pseudocode uses mathematical integers and separates sampling from evaluation:

\begin{verbatim}
require first sampled edge has reset asserted
for each rising edge:
    capture rst and en                # stimulus is already stable
    if rst: expected = 0
    else if en: expected = min(7, expected + 1)
    wait until registered updates settle
    compare q with expected           # unknown q also fails
\end{verbatim}

In an ordinary clocked simulation testbench one can drive \texttt{rst} and \texttt{en} at falling edges, then compare just before driving again at the following falling edge. This avoids racing a nonblocking register update at the rising edge. With reset followed by eight enabled edges, the expected visible values are \(0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,7\). Also check a disabled edge at a nonsaturated value, reset while enabled, and a fresh sequence after reset. The eighth increment attempt distinguishes saturation from wraparound.

One concurrent assertion, written in SystemVerilog inside a checker or bound module with these signals, reads:

\begin{verbatim}
assert property (@(posedge clk)
    (!rst && en && q == 3'd7) |=> (q == 3'd7));
\end{verbatim}

The consequent is sampled on the next rising edge before that edge's updates; it observes the result of the antecedent edge. This property alone does not show that \(7\) is reachable or that any lower state increments. A cover property or the directed saturation test checks that the antecedent is exercised. A particular formal frontend may support only a subset of SVA; consult the \href{https://yosyshq.readthedocs.io/projects/yosys/en/latest/using_yosys/more_scripting/model_checking.html}{Yosys model-checking guide} for its own supported flow rather than assuming every simulator assertion can be read by every synthesizer.

\section{Compare the Correct Level of Behavior}

For a combinational miter, share the inputs and OR the XORs of corresponding outputs. If asking whether the miter can be true is unsatisfiable, the Boolean functions agree over the input domain represented in the model. A sequential miter also has state on both sides, as in \Cref{fig:visual-320}. Here an inductive relation to the scoreboard is \(q=e\), where \(e\) is the integer reference state and \(0\le e\le7\). Reset establishes it. Below \(7\), the enabled sum is at most \(7\), so the HDL's fixed-width addition cannot wrap; at \(7\) and on disabled edges both states hold. This relation proves agreement after any reset, not only along the directed tests.

