An RTL-versus-mapped proof needs models for primitives, memory collision behavior, and relevant reset and initialization features. A retimed design can need a nontrivial state relation; a design with changed interface latency needs an explicitly realigned comparison. Abstracting a black box is sound only within its stated assumptions. Solver runtime, abstraction precision, and inadequate test coverage all limit the evidence. Finally, logical equivalence cannot rule out hold violations, electrical faults, or metastability. In particular, an equivalence miter treats a CDC synchronizer as ordinary Boolean state unless its clock and analog-resolution assumptions are modeled; it is not a CDC review. Keep timing, physical checks, and protocol verification as separate release criteria.
